6. SMART MONEY DETECTION ENGINE

The bot must monitor the wallet addresses that I manually add to enabled groups.

It must detect coordinated minting and purchasing activity across distinct tracked wallets.

Default mint rules
	•	Same verified collection.
	•	Same blockchain.
	•	Genuine mint activity.
	•	At least 3 distinct tracked wallets.
	•	Rolling 60-minute window.

Default purchase rules
	•	Same verified collection.
	•	Same blockchain.
	•	Genuine purchase activity.
	•	At least 3 distinct tracked wallets.
	•	Rolling 60-minute window.

Mint and purchase activity must be counted separately.

Immediate threshold alerts

When the third distinct qualifying wallet’s event is processed, the bot must evaluate the threshold and send the alert as soon as practical.

Do not wait for the 60-minute window to expire.

For example, if three distinct tracked wallets mint from the same verified collection on the same chain within the rolling window, trigger a mint alert.

If three distinct tracked wallets buy from that collection, trigger a separate purchase alert.

Distinct-wallet counting

Count each wallet only once globally for the relevant combination of:
	•	Collection.
	•	Chain.
	•	Activity type.
	•	Rolling time window.

Repeated events from the same wallet must not artificially increase the distinct-wallet count.

A wallet belonging to multiple groups counts only once globally.

Group-level breakdowns may show the same wallet in multiple groups, but global unique-wallet totals must remain deduplicated.

Clearly label global totals and group-level totals.

Rolling-window calculation

Use actual event timestamps provided by OpenSea.

A rolling 60-minute window must move continuously with time.

Handle:
	•	Out-of-order events.
	•	Duplicate events.
	•	Delayed events.
	•	Invalid timestamps.
	•	Events received after reconnection.
	•	Wallet removal.
	•	Group enablement and disablement.
	•	Configuration changes during monitoring.

Exclude events outside the configured window.

Do not use a fixed clock-hour calculation.

Correct activity classification

Do not count listings, offers, cancellations, ordinary transfers, or ambiguous events as purchases.

Count a purchase only when OpenSea’s documented event provides sufficient evidence that an actual purchase occurred.

Do not infer a mint from a transfer or sale.

If an event cannot be classified reliably, exclude it from the qualifying count and report the limitation.

NFT quantities

Only display NFT quantities when the available OpenSea event data supports them reliably.

Deduplicate events before aggregating quantities.

Do not assume one event always represents one NFT.

If quantity information is unavailable or unreliable, display NFT quantity unavailable or omit the quantity field.

